  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 AM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nThe monsoon has once again arrived across the area, I believe this \nis the third or fourth appearance so far. So far, it has behaved. We \ndo have some light showers across mainly the western half of the \narea but nothing heavy so far. The main concern in the forecast will \nbe the potential for heavy rain and possible strong thunderstorms \ntoday. Right now, in this meteorologists opinion, potential is mixed \nfor if we will see widespread of either.\n\nIn regards to heavy rain, the main factor will be the amount of \nmoisture in the atmosphere. Dew points have been slow to rise so \nfar, but they should this afternoon, rising into the 50s in portions \nof the Big Horn Basin as well as Johnson and Natrona Counties. \nPrecipitable water values should also be rather high. The GFS, for \nexample, has precipitable water values about 175 to 200 percent of \nnormal. This had been overdone by around 15 to 20 percent the past \nfew events though. Even with this, precipitable water values over \n150 percent of normal look fairly likely. There will also be some \nforcing in the form of a shortwave moving in from the west. This \nshould enhance chances for all areas, so everyone will have a \nchance. The best chance still looks to be in northwestern Wyoming, \ngenerally a 1 in 2 chance, and there is decent model agreement in \nthis. As for severe thunderstorms, there looks to be a decent amount \nof instability. This is especially so in the favored portions of our \narea, mainly East of the Bighorn Range in Johnson and Natrona \nCounties. Lifted indices fall as low as minus 4 to minus 5 with \nclose to 1500 J/Kg of surface CAPE here. There is also a decent \namount of directional shear in this area. This, plus the ample \nmoisture, may increase the hail threat in this area.  We do have a \nmarginal risk of severe weather in portions of Johnson and Natrona \nCounties, and this could be expanded westward with the 13Z update. \nAll areas could have a thunderstorm but with less instability and \nshear most storms would be sub severe.\n\nHowever, there are some factors against possible hazards. In regards \nto flooding and thunderstorms, it is cloud cover. Much of the area, \nespecially western Wyoming, is covered by clouds as I write this \naround midnight. If it stays mostly cloudy for much of the day, this \ncould limit instability somewhat and decrease the chance of stronger \nthunderstorms. There will likely be some areas that will be sunnier \nthough, especially further east. And there is one more mitigating \nfactor for heavy rain and possible flooding. With the shortwave \nmoving through, there will be some steering flow today. It won't be \nstrong, but most areas will have 15 to 25 knots at 500 millibars \nwhich should keep storms moving somewhat. We continue to have a \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall, and persistent storms could \nstill cause flooding. But it may decrease the chance somewhat. The \nshortwave should clear the area by midnight, but some showers will \nlikely linger through the night. As for temperatures, more cloud \ncover should lower them around 5 to 8 degrees, but it will still be \nabove normal for almost all areas.\n\nThe forecast from Sunday through much of next week looks fairly \nsimilar. We will remain on the edge of the ridge. Some monsoonal \nmoisture will stick around, with precipitable water values remaining \nabove average through much of the period. However, they do look \nlower than today, so the chance of convection should be somewhat \nlower.  Most guidance favors western Wyoming at this time. However, \ngiven the poor performance of the models in regards to timing and \nplacement of the showers and storms, confidence in the details \nremains low. Temperatures will also cool off somewhat, back down to \nnear to just slightly above normal levels for late August. There \ncontinue to be some indications of drier air moving in for the end \nof next week or next weekend, possibly shutting off the monsoon and \nbring a period of warm, but not really hot weather for the end of \nAugust.",
